{"skill":{"slug":"crypto-funding-alert","displayName":"Crypto Funding Alert"},"version":{"version":"1.0.1","createdAt":1772146232420,"changelog":"Improved documentation with detailed configuration guide and integration examples","changelogSource":"user","license":null,"files":[{"path":"README.md","size":711,"sha256":"dbf1e5dd9f4ac9ede790408c8a104bf094dee666b4de29f8d074fcd4fcfcf8d0","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":6175,"sha256":"c568913c9c80e40cbb473783c906b04b6c606c1b21cb528108d26f8867995b8c","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scan.js","size":7443,"sha256":"ba90686c36edda771614149f967c4a4b372ef1d490fbaf2b79d031542c1607d5","contentType":"text/javascript"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2488,"sha256":"a19105e59e776b3ead799a35152aeabdb720dd07347faa4d89c0531c1a266d13","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789086677309,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"0f0bd8b7fa15c0c8089dc9c00ee017d9f528979af3226bddbdf32062c6b66990","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/0f0bd8b7fa15c0c8089dc9c00ee017d9f528979af3226bddbdf32062c6b66990","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: crypto-funding-alert\nVersion: 1.0.1\n\nThe skill is classified as suspicious due to a potential uncontrolled file write vulnerability in `scan.js`. While the default data storage path is safe (`~/.openclaw/workspace/data/funding-monitor/scan_history.jsonl`), the `--output <path>` command-line argument allows writing the `scan_history.jsonl` file to an arbitrary directory. Although `path.join` is used, which mitigates simple path traversal, it still permits writing to any location the process has permissions for. This could be exploited by an attacker to write files to sensitive system directories if they can control the arguments passed to `scan.js`, leading to potential denial of service or other impacts. There is no evidence of intentional malicious behavior like data exfiltration or remote code execution within the provided code or prompt instructions.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779167321935},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":10,"checkedAt":1789086677309},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"medium","summary":"The skill appears purpose-built for crypto funding scans, but its financial safety claims are stronger than what the code actually enforces.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Network access to Binance public endpoints and local file writes are proportionate for a funding-rate monitor, but the Discord webhook example sends full scan output to a third party and should be treated as opt-in data sharing.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"note"},{"detail":"Installation is a normal skill install with a Node.js runtime requirement. No dependency install, remote code retrieval, tool wrapping, or privileged setup was found.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"The instructions describe scanning, local history, cron monitoring, and webhook examples. There is no prompt hijacking or hidden role-change behavior, but the documentation should more clearly separate informational output from enforced trading safeguards.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The script appends scan history and configuration to a local JSONL file by default under the OpenClaw workspace. This is disclosed in SKILL.md, but it is automatic and not shown in the CLI help as an opt-in choice.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The scanner fetches public Binance futures data, scores negative funding-rate opportunities, and prints trade-like recommendations, which fits the stated purpose; however, claimed safety controls such as leverage, stop-loss, position sizing, and max coin limits are mostly displayed configuration rather than enforced controls.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Install only if you treat this as an informational market scanner, not an automated trading advisor. Review the code before using the recommendations, avoid passing unsafe leverage or stop-loss values, be aware it saves scan history locally by default, and do not enable webhook examples unless you are comfortable sending the output to that service.","findings":"[TP4] expected: Local history and alert integrations are present and largely disclosed, so this is not tool poisoning by itself; the concern is under-emphasis in the top-level description.\n[AE1] expected: The referenced scan.js file was available in the workspace and was inspected during this review, so the partial-inspection concern is not upheld for this adjudication.\n[LP3] expected: The skill lacks explicit allowed-tools style scope metadata, but its practical authority is visible in the bundled script: public HTTPS requests and local history writes.\n[SQP-3] unexpected: The sample output contains Chinese labels, but the inspected scan.js prints English labels; this is a documentation quality issue rather than a security concern.\n[E1] expected: The Discord webhook is an optional integration example, not automatic exfiltration; users should understand it sends full scan output to an external service.\n[SDI-4] unexpected: This concern is supported: command-line values for leverage, stop-loss, and related settings are parsed without validation while the tool presents them as safety settings.\n[SDI-1] expected: Default local persistence is purpose-aligned for historical tracking and is disclosed in SKILL.md, but it is automatic and should be more visible in CLI help.\n[SQP-2] expected: The persisted data is scan history and configuration, not credentials, but it can reveal trading interests and should be configurable.\n[T09] unexpected: The static finding is supported in context: unvalidated financial parameters can produce unsafe or nonsensical recommendations while appearing to be validated safety controls.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789086677309}}}}}